\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Digital Lab: Sampling, Fourier Transforms, Mixers, and Down-Converters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
By Patrick Kantorski
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
March 7, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This report was written for a radio astronomy undergraduate course at UC
 Berkeley.
 For this lab, my partners and I examined and tested various concepts involved
 with digital signal processing.
 From Nyquist's criterion to signal recovery, we performed experiments and
 collected data.
 Additionally, digital and analog mixing (along with SSB and DSB mixing)
 was examined.
 We also provided a design for our FIR filter and test it by using a digital
 down converter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Analog signals can be converted to digital signals, but only at a specified
 sample rate and for a specified period of time.
 Many experiments in Radio Astronomy require recording and extracting informatio
n from radio signals.
 Although this is almost always done digitally, some parts of the signal
 mixing and filtering process can be performed with analog components.
 Signals possess many important properties and can even be mixed together
 and filtered out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this lab, my partners and I examined these concepts by using and implementin
g digital signal processing with a CPU based computer, function generators,
 ZAD-1 signal mixer, and an FPGA based computer called the ROACH.
 Plots and experimental results derived required extensive coding performed
 through the programming language Python.
 From Nyquist's criterion, signal sampling can be seen to have inherit limitatio
ns which must be considered to properly sample data.
 Additionally, we explored both analog and digital methods of signal mixing
 and compared the advantages and disadvantages of each.
 Techniques like as Fourier filtering can be used on signals in order to
 recover specific frequencies.
 Toward the end of this lab, my partners and I examined and programmed a
 FIR filter using the ROACH similar to that of a bandpass.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Materials & Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Nyquist's Criterion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Digital data collection functions by collecting sets of data points at a
 particular sample rate or sample frequency.
 One of the fundamental underlying concepts involved in digital data sampling
 is Nyquist's criterion.
 Nyquist's criterion for digital data collection states that there is a
 minimum sample frequency required to digitally sample a waveform in facsimile.
 To reproduce a sampled waveform, at least two data points must be taken
 per period.
 The Nyquist frequency is shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:nyquist"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{sample}=2\times v_{signal}\label{eq:nyquist}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because sampled points are separated in time, oscillations in the signal
 that occur between two data points will not be recorded.
 If Nyquist's criterion is not satisfied during data collection, aliasing
 will inevitably occur.
 Aliasing is when a sampled waveform facsimiles a lower frequency than what
 is being measured.
 An example of aliasing is shown in Figure 1.
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

