\end_inset

, was precisely
\begin_inset Formula $v_{signal}=0.5MHz$
\end_inset

, for observational purposes, we took data at signals ranging from 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{signal}=0.1MHz$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $0.9MHz$
\end_inset

.
 The sampled waveforms for these signals are shown in Figures 2 and 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fourier Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Fourier transform is a mathematical transformation used to transform signals
 between the time or spatial domain and frequency domain.
 In signal and data processing, Fourier transforms can be used to generate
 a power spectrum of a digital signal.
 Besides application, Fourier transforms are also very useful because sine
 waves are quite easy to work with mathematically and form a basis over
 all function spaces.
 Fourier transforms also distinguish negative and positive frequencies in
 a spectrum.
 A negative frequency is the same as a positive frequency except that instead
 of the cosine leading the sine by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

, it trails by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

 (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:complex"

\end_inset

).
 For functions with real-values, positive and negative frequencies contain
 the same information.
 In complex-valued functions, positive and negative frequencies contain
 independent information, making the transformation not centered about the
 origin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e^{i\omega t}=\cos(\omega t)+i\sin(\omega t)\label{eq:complex}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(v)=\frac{1}{T}\int_{-T/2}^{T/2}E(t)e^{2\pi jvt}dt, &  & E(t)=\frac{1}{F}\int_{-F/2}^{F/2}E(v)e^{-2\pi jvt}dt\label{eq:fourier}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fourier transforms are also invertible, allowing one to go from either go
 from the time to the frequency domain or from the frequency domain back
 to the time domain using the inverse transformation.
 In the proper analytic formulation (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:fourier"

\end_inset

), the boundaries of integration for the forward and inverse FT are from
 negative to positive infinity.
 However in application, these boundaries cannot be infinite.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two types of Fourier transforms were implemented and examined in this lab.
 One of them is known as the Discrete Fourier Transform or DFT.
 A DFT converts a finite list of equally spaced data samples taken from
 a function or signal into the list of coefficients of a finite combination
 of complex sine waves that has those same sample values, ordered by their
 frequencies.
 Since DFTs function with a finite amount of data, they can be implemented
 in computers by numerical algorithms or even dedicated hardware.
 Using the programming language Python, a DFT was constructed by my partners
 and I to view the power spectrum of the Nyquist sampling data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
DFTs have N input numbers and N output numbers, making and the number of
 calculations proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 However, for larger N values, the calculation can take a long time.
 Because of this, DFTs are inherently limited.
 To get around this problem, the Fast Fourier Transform, also known as the
 FFT, was developed as to perform Fourier transforms a lot quicker.
 Unlike a DFT, in a FFT, the number of calculations is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $N\log_{2}N$
\end_inset

.
 Since this makes larger N value transforms more practical, FFTs are typically
 used more often than DFTs.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Signal Mixing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While working with radio transmissions, desired signals are often at frequencies
 higher than what digital sampling tools are capable of handling.
 By using a heterodyne mixer, radio signals can be shifted down to a lower
 frequency so that they can be properly sampled.
 A local oscillator converts an incoming signal to a different frequency
 by mixing the signal with a local oscillated (or LO) signal.
 When two signals are mixed, their values are multiplied in the time domain.
 Additionally, this is equivalent to taking the convolution of the signals
 in the frequency domain (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:convolution"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(t)*g(t)=\int f(\omega)g(\omega)e^{i\omega t}d\omega\label{eq:convolution}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The convolution is a mathematical operation used in applications ranging
 from photo editing to radio astronomy.
 In simple terms, taking the convolution of two signals effectively 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

blurs
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or smears one over the other.
 In this lab, my partners and I examined both analog and digital mixing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To analog mixing our signals, we used a Mini-Circuits ZAD-1 and chose a
 specific LO frequency to mix along with another sine wave set at a different
 frequency (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:analog_mix"

\end_inset

).
 The ZAD-1 is a balanced mixer with identical “R” and “L” ports.
 It also does not couple to DC or very low frequencies.
 The “I” port is coupled differently and will handle voltages all the way
 down to and including DC.
 The mixing process functions no matter which two ports are used as inputs.
 We also had to make sure that we had enough samples per period to generate
 a reasonable facsimile of the sine wave.
 This forced us to sample above at least twice the Nyquist frequency and
 set the number of points sampled to be large enough to give produce at
 least a few periods of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

slowest
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 sine wave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{signal}=v_{lo}\pm\delta v\label{eq:analog_mix}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A single sideband mixer, or SSB, requires mixing with pure sine waves which
 have both a real and imaginary part.
 Because of this, SSB mixing removes half of the sidebands created.
 A double sideband mixer, or DSB, splits the signal into a lower sideband
 and an upper sideband.
 We first took our data samples at the mixed signal frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}+\delta v$
\end_inset

.
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}-\delta v$
\end_inset

.
 Since both signals were by the SRS Function Generator, they were inherently
 real and consisted of both 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{iωt}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-i\text{ω}t}$
\end_inset

 components (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:complex"

\end_inset

).
 This made the mixed output of our analog mixed signal composed of four
 different frequencies; 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}+\delta v$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}-\delta v$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $-v_{lo}+\delta v$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $-v_{lo}-\delta v$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Using the ROACH
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Reconfigurable Open Architecture for Computing Hardware (or ROACH) is
 an open-source board developed at the University of California, Berkeley
 by Dan Werthimer and Aaron Parsons from the CASPER collaboration.
 The circuit board of the ROACH used consists of a Virtex-5 FPGA made by
 Xilinx along with a CPU.
 The CPU allowing users to SSH directly to the board using a special linux
 kernel designed by CASPER.
 This kernel also allows for automatic interface support between programing
 the FPGA.
 
