	scale 55
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The picture above shows the data sampler program design used on the ROACH,
 written in the programming language Simulink.
 This design compiles data into the “adc snaps” BOF files which are used
 during digital mixing and sampling.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The ROACH is system constructed from a computer running a Linux kernel and
 a Field Programmable Gate Array (or FPGA) processor.
 Unlike CPU processors, FPGAs can be configured to perform multiple tasks
 simultaneously by a series of programable logic gates.
 In this lab, our FPGA processor was synchronized by a 200MHz clock.
 By connecting remotely, my partners and I programed the ROACH with configuratio
ns set to take data samples through altering the FPGA’s registers.
 Data taken from the ADC output is stored in the form of block random access
 memory (or BRAM).
 After being written, data then can be copied from the ROACH for speculation
 and analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
FIR Filtering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Finite Impulse Response filter (or FIR) works by taking the convolution
 of two signals in the time domain.
 Fourier filtering works well with filtering signals after they have been
 sampled, but not so much with signals in real-time.
 However, the digital implementation can still be achieved through programming
 a FPGA.
 By taking the Fourier transform of a signal, multiplying (or convolving)
 the FFTs by a filter, and then inverting transform to get the filtered
 signal, real-time digital filtration can be achieved (see Figure 3).
 
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
.
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /Users/ppkantorski/Documents/Radio_Astronomy/Lab_2/Pictures/FIR.png
	scale 34
	rotateOrigin center

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Figure above is a schematic diagram of how a FIR filter works.
 Coefficients or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

taps
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are represent by b
\begin_inset Formula $_{N}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Real-time digital filters perform a convolution on samples received as the
 signal is sampled.
 The implementation of an FIR filter on the FPGA allows us to convolve an
 incoming signal with other waveforms in the time domain.
 Because the filter my partners and I used has 8 taps, we decided to choose
 8 real and 8 imaginary coefficients for our filter.
 We did this by writing the coefficient registers to the FPGA.
 As the signal is sampled, the FPGA multiplies about 12 of the last eight
 points from the ADC with their corresponding coefficients and then sums
 them.
 As the clock cycles, the next point sampled is fed in and the process repeats.
 The values are then summed up to form a new 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

convolved
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 waveform.
 Since the sums are largest when the signal falls along the filter, the
 FIR filters out portions of the signal that are similar to the filter's
 shape and zeros out the rest.
\end_layout

