 For the positive 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}$
\end_inset

 frequencies, the output splits into a lower sideband
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}+v_{signal}$
\end_inset

 and an upper sideband 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}-v_{signal}$
\end_inset

, forming the double sideband (or DSB) mixer.
 Single sideband (or SSB) mixers require the mixing of pure sine waves that
 have both a real and imaginary part.
 With complex sine waves, we are able to eliminate one of the two sidebands.
 The upper and lower sideband of the mixed signal are 205kHz and 5kHz signals
 respectively.
 The upper and lower sideband are symmetric for the both negative and positive
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{lo}$
\end_inset

.
 
