\begin_layout Standard
Since the upper sideband distorts the 5kHz signal, I decided to filter it
 out.
 To do this, using Python, I made the values of each frequency higher on
 the FFT of the signal zero and then took the inverse FFT (or IFFT) to retrieve
 filtered signal.
 This was done in Python by first isolating 5kHz then using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

IFFT
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 feature built into the package 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

NumPy.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 This method of digital filtration is called Fourier filtering and can be
 seen in Figure 9.
 The band of a Fourier filter is somewhat arbitrary and really depends on
 some particular range of interest.
 In the data, we chose also prevented any mixing products that may have
 occurred as a result of any DC bias mixed into our signals.
 After isolating the 5kHz and taking the inverse Fourier transform of the
 power spectrum, the filtered signal was be retrieved from the mix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Digital Mixing (DSB and SSB) on an FPGA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
DSB Mixing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Digital DSB mixing is similar to analog DSB mixing because of how the signals
 are mixed.
 However, instead of mixing signals with physical, analog components, the
 mixing is performed digitally in a computer.
 But like analog mixing, there are still some inherit limitations.
 Even if the two signals being mixed together do not violate the Nyquist
 criteria individually, if the upper sideband of the signal is higher than
 the Nyquist frequency, then aliasing will still occur.
 Furthermore, if a DC offset is present in any of the separate signals,
 then it will also show up as spikes on the power spectrum of the mixing
 product around their frequencies.
 
\end_layout

