\begin_layout Subsubsection
SSB Mixing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although DSB mixing is useful, it can become problematic when mixing involves
 a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 local oscillator signal.
 Single sideband (or SSB) mixing is one method of getting around this problem.
 Real signals require a superposition of the sine wave incorporating both
 of the positive and negative frequencies (see Figure 12).
 SSB mixing works by taking a signal and splitting it into two identical
 signals, one a cosine wave and another a sine wave.
 The cosine mixed signal is then treated as the real component of the mixed
 signal while the mixed sine wave represents the magnify components.
 The power spectrum of this mix is shown in Figure 13.
 
\end_layout

