\begin_layout Standard
To choose coefficients for our FIR filter, we first designed our bandpass
 in the frequency domain and then apply the IFFT of the bandpass to retrieve
 our signal shape in the time domain.
 Since we did this digitally, we chose 8 points on the filter so that the
 IFFT would generate a complex coefficient for each of the eight registers.
 The filter we constructed for this lab is called a 5/8-filter.
 We call this a 5/8-filter because it was designed to filter the lower 5/8
 portion of the frequencies that the FFT can represent.
 Additionally, because filter is designed to be symmetric about 0Hz, all
 of the filter's coefficients had to be real.
 Since our FPGA's clock or sample rate is 200MHz, the Nyquist frequency
 of our sampling is about 100 MHz.
 This made our desired frequency range from about −100MHz to 100MHz.
 The full response of our 5/8-band filter is 5/8 of this range; from −62.5
 MHz to 62.5 MHz.
 The coefficients calculated for the 5/8 filter can be found in Table 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The program we used on the ROACH was set to handle 18-bit fixed point numbers.
 The numbers listed in the “Decimal Floating Point” column had to be converted
 to hex-digits representing each bit for the fixed point numbers.
 This is done by converting filter coefficients into an integer that have
 the same bits as the 18-bit fixed point representation.
 Using the bits contained in the integer, the hex-digits values were then
 used to control the ROACH.
 Using Python, my partners and I generated both hex digits used to control
 the ROACH along with the binary representations of the filter coefficients.
 Because there are only 8 coefficients, the coefficients alone do not provide
 a fine frequency resolution.
 To get the prediction of the response of our filter have a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

finer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 frequency resolution, my partners and I placed zeroes on the end of the
 list for the filter's coefficients, virtually increasing our sample time.
