\begin_layout Section
Conclusion 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Signal processing is used in many applications and fields of study, especially
 Radio Astronomy.
 Through testing the Nyquist criterion, my partners and I found that frequencies
 up to one-half the sampling rate can be accurently facsimliated.
 In this lab, we digitally sampled signals, analog and digitally mixed signals,
 performed various methods of filtration, and examined the FIR filter response
 set by the ROACH.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, my partners and I examined both analog and digital methods
 of DSB and SSB signal mixing.
 We were also able to make comparisons with the advantages and disadvantages
 of each.
 Through using techniques like Fourier filtering, digital filtration was
 made possible.
 Since digital mixing and signal filtration are used regularly in Radio
 Astronomy, understanding just what exactly these signals are and how they
 can be filtered is extremely relative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the end, I also learned about another method of digital filtering in
 the down conversion process using a FIR filter.
 FIR filters allow us to filter out unwanted frequencies from a DSB signal
 mixer.
 However, we were limited by the number of taps or coefficients used to
 convolve our signal.
 For purposes of isolating a certain frequency, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

rippled
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 shape of our the filter response (see Figure 15) will worked well since
 the response eliminated frequencies outside our bandwidth.
 By combining a FIR filter set with a specified sampling rate and using
 a heterodyne mixer, radio signals can be converted into useful forms for
 analysis.
\end_layout

